4. Known gap, flagged plainly rather than quietly shipped
------------------------------------------------------------------------
A checkout that reserves credit and is then silently abandoned — never
completes, and PayFast never sends a FAILED/CANCELLED ITN either (the
business just closes the tab) — leaves that reservation at 'reserved'
indefinitely, with its amount unavailable to other payments until
released. Retrying checkout for the SAME request is safe (the
reservation is idempotent per payment_id, so it returns the existing
figures rather than reserving again) — this only affects credit that's
never retried and never explicitly fails. A scheduled sweep releasing
stale 'reserved' rows after some window would close this properly.
Building that would mean a new scheduled-job system within an already
large fix (the same reasoning schema_phase87 used for not rebuilding a
full grace-period lifecycle for Featured Placement), so it wasn't built
here — flagged in the migration's own header comment and in DEPLOY.md
instead of left to be discovered later.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. Why this shape, not a different one
------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Reserve/confirm/release, not a single atomic deduct-at-checkout step,
  specifically to keep schema_phase55's own "don't touch the balance
  until PayFast confirms" principle intact for the one branch where it
  still meaningfully applies (the partial-coverage, redirect-to-PayFast
  path), while still closing the race for the branch where that
  principle can't apply at all (the fully-covered, immediate-finalize
  path).
- A new dedicated table (launch_credit_redemptions) rather than trying to
  infer redemption history from payments.credit_applied alone, because
  the finding explicitly asks for "an explicit reservation/redemption
  record" and "a payment-to-credit relation" — payments.credit_applied on
  its own can't distinguish reserved from confirmed from released, or
  guarantee a payment was only ever redeemed against once.
- Fixed the amount-mismatch bug in the same pass rather than filing it
  separately, because it sat directly in the code path this fix needed
  to touch anyway (the exact "expected" value payfast-notify checks
  against), and the redesign would have been silently non-functional for
  every partial-credit payment without also fixing it.

